course: dictionary
lesson: 3445
title: Jokes at the wrong time
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: Facetious is a grown-up word, so read the clues around it. Someone jokes when something serious is happening, and the joke seems silly or rude. «Don't be facetious» is what a teacher might say when a pupil jokes instead of answering properly.
explain: «She made a facetious remark when the class was upset.» The clues are the upset class and the word remark. A joke at a sad moment is at the wrong time, so facetious means joking at the wrong time. Test it: put «a silly joke at the wrong time» in place of the hard word, and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Facetious is an adjective, and its adverb is facetiously: «He answered facetiously.» The adverb tells you how he answered, which is the same kind of clue as in the earlier lessons.
fact: Facetious means joking at the wrong time, about something serious, so it seems silly or rude.
fact: «A facetious remark» is a joke made when it should not be.
fact: «Don't be facetious» is a common warning from a teacher to a pupil who jokes instead of answering seriously.
fact: Facetious is a formal, grown-up word, and its adverb is facetiously.
fact: Spelling fun: «facetious» has the vowels a, e, i, o, u once each, in alphabetical order, and so does «abstemious». This is about spelling, not meaning.
exercise: «She made a facetious remark when the class was upset.» What does facetious mean? | Happy to help | *Joking at the wrong time, so it seems silly or rude | Quiet and shy | Very serious and careful
exercise: The teacher said, «Don't be facetious» when Sam joked during the test. What did the teacher want? | Sam to keep joking | Sam to sit down quietly | *Sam to stop joking and answer seriously | Sam to tell the class a story
exercise: «He answered facetiously, and the head teacher frowned.» Find and copy the one word that tells how he answered. | head | *facetiously | answered | frowned
test: In «He spoke facetiously», what kind of word is facetiously? | A question word | A noun | *An adverb | A colour
test: Which situation shows a facetious remark? | A pupil says thank you to the teacher | *A pupil jokes about a friend's injury right after it happens | A pupil reads a book quietly | A pupil shares a joke with friends at lunch
exam: Which statement about facetious is true? | It means very old, like the word ancient | It means giving out light, like the word luminous | It means quiet and gloomy, like the word glum | *It means joking at the wrong time, about something serious
